# NeuroBoost Project Rules

## Project Overview
This is the NeuroBoost project. When working on this codebase:

- Follow modern development best practices
- Write clean, maintainable code with proper documentation
- Use consistent naming conventions
- Include appropriate error handling
- Add comments for complex logic

## General Guidelines
- Always consider performance implications
- Write tests for new functionality
- Follow the existing code style and patterns
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Keep functions focused and single-purpose

## Code Quality
- Prefer readability over cleverness
- Use descriptive commit messages
- Document public APIs and interfaces
- Handle edge cases appropriately
- Validate inputs where necessary

# .cursorrules for NeuroBoost ADHD Productivity Web App

# Architecture
- Maintain modular, agent-based architecture:
  - TaskAgent, MoodAgent, FocusAgent, MotivateAgent
- Agents must use event-based communication (pub/sub or message bus)
- Do not create tight coupling between agents or hardcoded interdependencies

# Frontend (React + TypeScript)
- Use functional components with hooks (no class components)
- Use TailwindCSS for all styling (no inline styles or external CSS files)
- Respect mood-responsive theming using emotion-based variables (e.g., bg-mood-calm)
- Components must support dark/light mode with a toggle context
- Avoid third-party UI libraries unless scoped and justified
- Keep UI adaptive across mobile, desktop, and tablet (PWA-first design)

# Backend (Node/Express or similar)
- Use TypeScript
- Define all routes and handlers in modular route/controller format
- Validate all API inputs (e.g., with Zod or Joi)
- Do not use monolithic server files

# Personal Knowledge Graph (PKG)
- Centralize task, emotion, focus, and goal data in a dedicated `pkg/` service
- No duplicated user state outside the PKG unless derived for temporary use
- Use indexedDB or local-first database for local graph

# Gamification + Analytics
- Task completions must emit XP/incentive events
- Log emotional/focus state for time-series analytics
- All analytics logs should be structured (JSON) and routed to a `logger/` module

# Assistant & Voice Integration
- Abstract assistant API (Google/Siri/Alexa) into a unified `assistant.ts` interface
- Do not hardcode vendor-specific logic in components

# CBT Tools
- Store CBT scripts and breathing prompts in structured JSON or Markdown
- Keep language compassionate, non-directive, and opt-in

# Inference Rules
- Run local LLM by default; cloud fallback must handle latency and failover
- Fallback logic must include user notification and transparency

# File + Folder Conventions
- Use PascalCase for components and agents (e.g., `FocusAgent.ts`)
- Place agents in `agents/` directory, utilities in `utils/`, and routes in `routes/`
- Never modify `/legacy/` or `/experimental/` directories
- Never commit `.env` files or secret keys

# Style & Linting
- Use Prettier and ESLint with project config (`.eslintrc.js`, `.prettierrc`)
- Prefer named exports over default exports
- Enforce strict types — no `any` unless unavoidable with TODO

# Cursor Instructions
- When generating code:
  - Use Tailwind for UI
  - Use event-driven communication for logic
  - Maintain modular structure
  - Keep edits safe and scoped
- Do not modify unrelated files when responding to prompts
- Avoid auto-writing to `tests/` unless explicitly asked

# Naming
- Variables: camelCase
- Components/Agents: PascalCase
- Events: onEventName

# Comments & Docs
- Comment logic for adaptive scheduling, emotion tracking, and context shifts
- Provide inline guidance when logic depends on mood/emotion state
